  "phase": "IDLE",
  "instruction": "No active spec. If the user described what they want, run `npx eser@latest noskills spec new \"description\"` immediately; the name is auto-generated. Present ALL available concerns (split across multiple calls if needed).",
  "welcome": "noskills is a state-machine orchestrator that acts as a scrum master for both you and your agent — keeping work focused, decisions in your hands, and tokens efficient.",
  "existingSpecs": [
    {
      "name": "add-login-page",
      "phase": "DISCOVERY",
      "iteration": 0
    }
  ],
  "availableConcerns": [
    {
      "id": "open-source",
      "description": "Community-driven, inclusive, well-documented. Prioritize contributor experience, avoid exclusionary patterns, default to permissive choices."
    },
    {
      "id": "beautiful-product",
      "description": "Design and UX are first-class. Agent critiques UI/UX like a passionate designer. Empty states, error states, loading states, mobile layout — nothing ships unspecified."
    },
    {
      "id": "long-lived",
      "description": "Built to last. Stability, maintainability, and backward compatibility matter. Every shortcut needs justification. Favor boring technology."
    },
    {
      "id": "move-fast",
      "description": "Optimize for shipping speed. Acceptable trade-offs on polish. Good enough is good enough. Reduce ceremony."
    },
    {
      "id": "compliance",
      "description": "Audit trails, access controls, data handling rules. Every change is traceable. Verification is mandatory, not optional."
    },
    {
      "id": "learning-project",
      "description": "Experimentation is the goal. Encourage trying new patterns, don't punish dead ends. Document learnings over polish."
    },
    {
      "id": "well-engineered",
      "description": "Engineering craftsmanship. Performance is measured, not assumed. Tests are strategic, not ceremonial. Errors are helpful, not cryptic. Security is a design constraint, not a checklist. Observability is built-in, not bolted on. The system is debuggable by someone who didn't write it."
    },
    {
      "id": "developer-experience",
      "description": "APIs and CLIs are products. Time-To-Hello-World is the north star metric. Friction at any step is a bug. Progressive disclosure, sensible defaults, and honest error messages are non-negotiable."
    },
    {
      "id": "root-cause",
      "description": "No fix without a root cause investigation first. Symptoms are not bugs: the bug is the condition that allowed the symptom to occur. A fix that addresses the symptom without finding the root cause leaves that condition in place, so the bug comes back. Every bugfix traces the failure to its origin and verifies the chain."
    },
    {
      "id": "qa-tested",
      "description": "Adversarial QA is not 'clicking around'. It is systematic exploration with the mindset of a user who doesn't know how the system works and is trying to break it. Happy path only is not QA. Every input boundary, every empty state, every error path must be verified. Ship nothing that hasn't been QA'd."
    },
    {
      "id": "security-audited",
      "description": "Security is a design constraint, not a post-ship checklist. Every trust boundary, every user-controlled input, every secret, every endpoint is a potential attack surface. The job is to enumerate the attack surface completely, then verify each vector. OWASP Top 10 is the minimum bar, not the ceiling."
    },
    {
      "id": "peer-reviewed",
      "description": "Code review is not approval-seeking. It is adversarial verification by someone who cares about the outcome. A good reviewer finds what the author missed — not to be difficult, but because the author is too close to the code to see its failure modes. Critical findings block merge. Informational findings inform. Both require a response."
    },
    {
      "id": "ship-ready",
      "description": "Ship readiness is not about whether the feature works. It is about whether the artifact is ready for production: tests passing, coverage adequate, scope contained, changelog updated, version correct, and no outstanding hygiene debt. A feature that works but leaves the repo in worse shape is not ready to ship."
    },
    {
      "id": "documented",
      "description": "Documentation is not a post-ship task. It is part of the definition of done. If a feature ships without documentation, it does not exist for the user who finds it six months later. Every public API change, every CLI flag change, every behavior change needs a corresponding documentation update. The diff in code must have a diff in docs."
    },
    {
      "id": "deploy-ready",
      "description": "Deploy readiness is not about whether the code is correct. It is about whether the infrastructure, configuration, and pipeline are ready to receive it. A correct feature with a broken deploy config ships nothing. Verify the config before the merge — not after."
    },
    {
      "id": "production-ready",
      "description": "Production readiness is measured in how fast you can detect and recover from failure — not whether failure is possible. Every system fails. The question is whether you know about it in minutes or days, and whether you can recover in minutes or days. Observability, alerting, and runbooks are not optional. They are the difference between a production system and a production disaster waiting to happen."
    },
    {
      "id": "retro-fed",
      "description": "The retrospective is where compounding learning happens. A spec completed without a retro is a missed opportunity to get better. The questions are not 'what went wrong' — they are 'what would you do differently', 'where was the model wrong', 'what did you learn that should change how you approach the next one'. Learnings that stay in one person's head compound for one person. Documented learnings compound for the team."
    }
  ],
  "activeConcerns": [],
  "activeRulesCount": 0,
  "behavioralNote": "These options are fallbacks. If the user already described what they want, act on it directly without presenting these options.",
  "hint": "No concerns active. Consider adding concerns first — they shape discovery questions and specs.",
  "meta": {
    "protocol": "Run `npx eser@latest noskills next --answer=\"...\"` to submit results and advance",
    "spec": null,
    "branch": null,
    "iteration": 0,
    "lastProgress": null,
    "activeConcerns": [],
    "resumeHint": "No active spec. Start one with: `npx eser@latest noskills spec new --name=<slug> \"description\"`",
    "enforcement": {
      "level": "enforced",
      "capabilities": [
        "PreToolUse file edit gate",
        "Git write guard",
        "Stop iteration tracking",
        "PostToolUse file logging",
        "Sub-agent delegation"
      ]
    }
  },
  "behavioral": {
    "rules": [
      "If the user described a feature/bug/task, create a spec immediately: `npx eser@latest noskills spec new \"description\"`; the name is auto-generated. Do NOT present menus or ask 'What would you like to do?' unless the conversation has no prior context.",
      "NEVER run git write commands (commit, add, push, checkout, stash, reset, merge, rebase, cherry-pick). Git is read-only for agents. The user controls git. You may read: git log, git diff, git status, git show, git blame.",
      "Report progress honestly. Not done = 'not done'. Partial = 'partial: [works]/[doesn't]'. Untested = 'untested'. 4 of 6 = '4 of 6 done, 2 remaining'.",
      "Never skip steps or infer decisions. Use AskUserQuestion for all decision points. Recommend first, then ask.",
      "Display `roadmap` before other content. Display `gate` prominently.",
      "NEVER suggest bypassing, skipping, or 'breaking out of' noskills. Discovery helps the user — it is not an obstacle. If scope changes: revise spec, reset and create new, or split.",
      "Run the next noskills command as soon as the current step is done; each step has exactly one next step, so there is nothing to ask permission for.",
      "Listen first: after spec creation, ask 'Tell me about this. Share as much context as you have.' Wait for their response before mode selection. Rich context (>200 chars) → suggest pre-filled discovery answers from it, marked STATED or INFERRED for the user to confirm. Brief response → proceed normally.",
      "Discovery questions are adaptive. Once the user has answered the base questions, write 1-3 follow-up questions for each answer that reveals ambiguity, risk, dependencies, or missing detail, and register them with `npx eser@latest noskills spec <name> followup <questionId> \"question\"` before you submit the answers (max 3 per question). noskills then gives you the follow-ups one at a time.",
      "Confidence scoring: every technical finding needs a confidence score (1-10). 9-10: verified (read code, ran test). 7-8: strong evidence. 5-6: reasonable inference. 3-4: guess. 1-2: speculation. State basis ('read X', 'inferred from Y'). If confidence < 5, prefix with '⚠ Unverified:'.",
      "Pass interactiveOptions DIRECTLY to AskUserQuestion options array (header max 12 chars). Use commandMap to resolve selections. For availableConcerns: AskUserQuestion with multiSelect:true, max 4 per question — split across questions if needed. Present ALL concerns.",
      "Encourage full context: 'Tell me what you want to build: a one-liner, detailed requirements, meeting notes, anything.' Slug is auto-generated. Pass full text to `npx eser@latest noskills spec new \"...\"`.",
      "After spec new, run next and present the discovery mode options it returns; never skip discovery without asking.",
      "Every task gets a spec. No exceptions. A one-liner fix, a config change, a 'simple' refactor — all get specs. The spec can be short but it must exist. 'Too simple for a spec' is the anti-pattern.",
      "SPEC CREATION: When the user says 'create a new spec', 'new spec', or similar, do NOT run `npx eser@latest noskills spec new` without a description. Ask the user 'What do you want to build?' via AskUserQuestion first. Wait for their response. Then run: `npx eser@latest noskills spec new \"their full response\"`.",
      "CONCERN MANAGEMENT: When the user wants to add or view concerns, run `npx eser@latest noskills concern list` immediately. Do NOT analyze or recommend concerns. Present the list via AskUserQuestion (multiSelect:true, max 4 per question). Use a SINGLE `npx eser@latest noskills concern add id1 id2 id3` command for all selections."
    ],
    "tone": "Welcoming. Present choices, then wait."
  },
  "roadmap": "[ IDLE ] → DISCOVERY → REFINEMENT → PROPOSAL → APPROVED → EXECUTING → COMPLETED → IDLE",
  "modeDirective": "No active spec. Plan mode optional.",
  "interactiveOptions": [
    {
      "label": "Add concerns (Recommended)",
      "description": "Shape how discovery and specs work by adding project concerns"
    },
    {
      "label": "Start a new spec",
      "description": "Tell me what you want to build: a one-liner, detailed requirements, meeting notes, anything"
    },
    {
      "label": "Continue: add-login-page (DISCOVERY)",
      "description": "Iteration 0"
    }
  ],
  "commandMap": {
    "Add concerns (Recommended)": "npx eser@latest noskills concern add <id> [<id2> ...]",
    "Start a new spec": "npx eser@latest noskills spec new \"description\"",
    "Continue: add-login-page (DISCOVERY)": "npx eser@latest noskills spec add-login-page next"
  },
  "toolHint": "AskUserQuestion",
  "toolHintInstruction": "Present these options with AskUserQuestion so the user's pick maps to `commandMap`."
}

